o Complete man/writeZarrArray-auto-args.Rd

o ZarrArraySeed() and ZarrArray() should support Zarr datasets on S3.
  Note that Rarr::read_zarr_array(), the workhorse behind the extract_array()
  method for ZarrArraySeed objects, already supports datasets stored on S3.

o writeZarrArray() needs to write the array dimnames to disk.

o Add support for sparse Zarr datasets.

o Improve support for Zarr v3:

  Some of the known issues with current support of Zarr v3 in Rarr:

  - Rarr::read_zarr_array() chokes when using an "empty index" on a Zarr v3
    dataset. For example:

      library(Rarr)

      zarr_v2 <- system.file(package="Rarr", "extdata",
                             "zarr_examples", "column-first", "int32.zarr")
      read_zarr_array(zarr_v2, list(integer(0), integer(0), integer(0))) # ok
      read_zarr_array(zarr_v2, list(integer(0), NULL, 1))                # ok

      zarr_v3 <- system.file(package="Rarr", "extdata",
                             "zarr_examples", "column-first", "int32_v3.zarr")
      read_zarr_array(zarr_v3, list(integer(0), integer(0), integer(0)))# error!
      read_zarr_array(zarr_v3, list(integer(0), NULL, 1))               # error!

  - Rarr::create_empty_zarr_array() only supports creation of Zarr v2
    datasets at the moment (Rarr 1.11.24). As a consequence, writeZarrArray(),
    which is based on Rarr::create_empty_zarr_array(), can only write in Zarr
    v2 format at the moment.


